; todo 38: a sorted schema variable that can't match an unsorted value used to fail completely
; silently -- `variable_sorts_allow` (inside `unify_exprs_with_patterns`) just returns `False`,
; the same way a genuine structural mismatch does, so the rule was simply absent from the search's
; candidates, with "no rule with a matching conclusion was found" giving no hint why. Found while
; building proofs/logic-encodings/second-order-leibniz.kurt (dev/todo.md #36).
;
; Fix: `failure_explanation`'s diagnostic pass (only reached after a line has already failed, never
; the live search) retries a non-matching rule's conclusion with sorts ignored (`ignoring_sorts`);
; if that succeeds, `sort_violations` reports exactly which schema variable needed which sort, and
; what unsorted value it was given instead.

sort obj
const a   ; deliberately NOT given sort `obj` -- that's the point

var x
obj x

arity P 1
arity Q 1
use (Q x) implies (P x) "p-rule"
use Q x "q-always"

; `a` has no declared sort, so `p-rule`'s `x` (sort `obj`) can't bind to it -- the old message gave
; no hint why; the new one names the rule, the variable, the required sort and the unsorted value
expect "ProofError" "`p-rule` (line 20): `x` needs sort `obj`, but `a` has no declared sort"
    P a
